.TH BITCALC 1 "@DATE@" "bitcalc @VERSION@" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
bitcalc \- bitwise operations, binary arithmetic and base conversion with step\-by\-step visualisation
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B bitcalc
[\fIFLAGS\fR] \fINUMBER\fR
.br
.B bitcalc
[\fIFLAGS\fR] \fINUMBER\fR \fIOPERATOR\fR \fINUMBER\fR
.br
.B bitcalc
[\fIFLAGS\fR] \fIUNARY_OPERATOR\fR \fINUMBER\fR
.br
.B bitcalc
[\fIFLAGS\fR] \fINUMBER\fR \fIUNARY_OPERATOR\fR
.br
.B echo
"\fIEXPRESSION\fR" |
.B bitcalc
[\fIFLAGS\fR]
.br
.B bitcalc \-i
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B bitcalc
is a hardware\-engineering calculator. It treats every number as a fixed\-width
register, shows the working as aligned bit rows (carries, shifted\-out bits, flags,
truth tables) and can print just the answer for use in scripts.
.PP
When standard output is a terminal the working is shown. When it is not (a pipe or
a redirect) only the result is printed, exactly as with
.BR \-\-quiet ,
unless
.B \-\-verbose
is given.
.SS Number syntax
Prefixes select the base: \fB0b\fR1010 (binary), \fB0o\fR17 (octal), \fB0x\fR1F (hexadecimal);
no prefix means decimal unless \fB\-\-from\fR is given. A leading \fB\-\fR makes the value
negative (two's complement). Decimal input may have a fractional part (\fB13.625\fR);
binary input with \fB\-\-from bin\fR may too (\fB1101.101\fR).
.PP
Hexadecimal digits can spell operator names (ADD, AND, ...). Use the \fB0x\fR prefix
for such values.
.SS Width
The register width is the smallest of 8, 16, 32 or 64 bits that holds every operand,
unless \fB\-\-width\fR is given. Results wrap to the width and the
.B flags
row reports Z (zero), N (sign bit), C (carry, or B for borrow on SUB) and V (signed overflow).
.SS Signed and unsigned display
Decimal results are shown unsigned unless an operand is negative (or the operation is
NEG, or SUB borrowed), in which case they are shown signed. The other interpretation is
always printed next to it. \fB\-\-unsigned\fR forces the unsigned view.
.SH FLAGS
.TP
\fB\-f\fR, \fB\-\-from\fR \fIBASE\fR
Input base: bin, oct, dec, hex (or 2, 8, 10, 16).
.TP
\fB\-t\fR, \fB\-\-to\fR \fIBASE\fR
Output base: bin, oct, dec, hex, bcd, gray. Default: dec.
.TP
\fB\-w\fR, \fB\-\-width\fR \fIBITS\fR
Register width, 2 to 64. \fBauto\fR restores automatic selection.
.TP
\fB\-\-prec\fR \fIN\fR
Fractional bits produced when converting a decimal fraction to binary. Default: 8.
.TP
\fB\-q\fR, \fB\-\-quiet\fR
Print only the final value.
.TP
\fB\-v\fR, \fB\-\-verbose\fR
Always print the working, even when output is not a terminal.
.TP
\fB\-T\fR, \fB\-\-truth\fR
Add a truth table (logic operators, NOT) or a bit\-mapping table (shifts and rotates).
With only a logic operator and no operands, prints its plain truth table.
.TP
\fB\-u\fR, \fB\-\-unsigned\fR
Show decimal results unsigned.
.TP
\fB\-\-no\-color\fR, \fB\-\-color\fR
Disable or force colour. The NO_COLOR environment variable is honoured.
.TP
\fB\-i\fR, \fB\-\-interactive\fR
Start the interactive shell (see INTERACTIVE MODE).
.TP
\fB\-V\fR, \fB\-\-version\fR
Print the version.
.TP
\fB\-h\fR, \fB\-\-help\fR
Print a short usage summary.
.SH OPERATORS
Operators are case\-insensitive. Symbols may be used for the common ones; quote
.BR '*' ,
.BR '&' ,
.BR '|' ,
.BR '<<'
and
.BR '>>'
in the shell.
.TP
.B Arithmetic
ADD (+), SUB (\-), MUL (*), DIV (/), MOD (%).
Division and remainder use signed values when an operand is negative and unsigned values otherwise;
unsigned 64\-bit operands above 2^63\-1 are not supported for DIV and MOD.
.TP
.B Logic
AND (&), OR (|), XOR (^), NAND, NOR, XNOR.
.TP
.B Shifts
LSHIFT (<<), RSHIFT (>>, logical), ASR (>>>, arithmetic, copies the sign bit).
A shift count equal to or larger than the width is an error.
.TP
.B Rotates
ROL, ROR. The count is taken modulo the width.
.TP
.B Direction of negative counts
A negative count runs the opposite operation: LSHIFT \-n is RSHIFT n, RSHIFT \-n is LSHIFT n,
ROL \-n is ROR n, ROR \-n is ROL n, and ASR \-n is LSHIFT n. The working states when this happens.
.TP
.B Unary
NOT (~) and 1COMP flip every bit.
2COMP shows the two's\-complement bit pattern of a number (a negative number is built by inverting its
magnitude and adding one; a non\-negative number is unchanged).
NEG negates (invert, add one).
BSWAP reverses byte order (width 16, 24, ... 64).
POPCNT counts 1 bits, PARITY is that count modulo 2, CLZ and CTZ count leading and trailing zero bits,
REV mirrors the bit order.
.SH INTERACTIVE MODE
.B bitcalc \-i
starts a shell with history (kept in
.IR $XDG_STATE_HOME/bitcalc/history ).
Any expression or flag combination accepted on the command line works, plus:
.TP
.B ans
the previous result.
.TP
\fB:width\fR \fIN|auto\fR, \fB:to\fR \fIBASE\fR, \fB:from\fR \fIBASE|auto\fR
change settings for the session.
.TP
\fB:truth\fR \fIon|off\fR, \fB:verbose\fR \fIon|off\fR, \fB:unsigned\fR \fIon|off\fR
toggle options.
.TP
.B :state, help, clear, exit
show settings, help, clear the screen, quit (also \fB:q\fR).
.SH EXAMPLES
.TP
Show the working for a bitwise AND with a negative operand:
bitcalc 10 AND \-2
.TP
Hexadecimal result of an addition in a pipeline:
bitcalc \-q 10 LSHIFT 2 | bitcalc \-t hex
.TP
Rotate; the negative count rotates the other way and a bit\-mapping table is added:
bitcalc \-T 0b10110001 ROL \-3
.TP
Byte\-swap a 16\-bit value:
bitcalc \-w 16 0xABCD BSWAP
.TP
Bit pattern of a negative number in hexadecimal:
bitcalc \-w 16 2COMP \-10 \-\-to hex
.TP
Decimal fraction to binary with 12 fractional bits:
bitcalc \-\-prec 12 13.625
.TP
Truth table of XNOR:
bitcalc \-T XNOR
.TP
Binary coded decimal:
bitcalc \-\-to bcd 94
.SH EXIT STATUS
0 on success, 1 on any error (message on standard error).
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B NO_COLOR
Disable colour output.
.TP
.B XDG_STATE_HOME
Location of the interactive history (default ~/.local/state).
.SH AUTHOR
Nyang'ada Stone Bradley
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR bash (1)
